  "faqs": [
    {
      "q": "What is human-in-the-loop in agentic AI?",
      "a": "It is a design where an AI agent pauses at selected steps, usually before a sensitive action, and waits for a person to approve, edit or reject what it proposes. The agent continues only with that human decision, so high-impact actions are never taken on the model's judgement alone."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which AI agent platforms support human approval before sensitive actions?",
      "a": "LangGraph (interrupts), LangChain (HumanInTheLoopMiddleware), n8n (human review on AI Agent tools), CrewAI (the @human_feedback decorator in Flows), the OpenAI Agents SDK (needs_approval), Microsoft Agent Framework (ApprovalRequiredAIFunction) and Google ADK (tool confirmation) all support it. Check each project's docs, as features change often."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I add an approval step in LangGraph?",
      "a": "Call interrupt() in a node or tool before the risky action, compile the graph with a checkpointer, run it with a thread_id, show the interrupt payload to a reviewer and resume with Command(resume=decision) on the same thread_id. Put the actual write after the interrupt, ideally in its own node."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can n8n wait for human approval before an AI agent updates a business system?",
      "a": "Yes. Add a Human review step to the AI Agent's tools panel, pick a channel such as Slack, Teams, Gmail or WhatsApp, and connect only the risky tools to it. The workflow pauses until someone approves or denies the tool call."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between human in the loop and human on the loop?",
      "a": "Human in the loop means a person must decide before certain actions run. Human on the loop means the system acts on its own while people monitor it and can intervene. Use in-the-loop for irreversible or external actions, and on-the-loop for low-risk, easily reversed work."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agent actions should require human approval?",
      "a": "Anything that sends external messages, moves money, deletes data, deploys code or changes important business records such as credit limits, prices or order status. Read-only lookups and easily reversible internal drafts usually do not need approval, but should still be logged."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does adding human approval make an AI agent secure?",
      "a": "No, it is one layer. Also limit which tools the agent has, give each tool the minimum permissions, enforce authorisation in the target system, defend against prompt injection and keep an audit log. Approval catches mistakes the other layers miss."
    }
  ],
